From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zaid <283011337+nottzaid@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 19:22:46 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Water depth colour and wind ripples

PBRAppearance.attenuationColor and attenuationDistance (glTF
KHR_materials_volume): light through a transmissive surface turns into
attenuationColor every attenuationDistance metres of its path to what
lies behind, found from the opaque scene's depth copy. scatterColor:
the colour deep water takes under white light, lit as a diffuse
surface where the path is long. Shallows show their bed; deep water
its own colour. waves: the slope of wind ripples, gradient-noise
octaves from 3 m down to 0.1 m in world space, each drifting downwind
at deep water's speed for its length, in gusts that drift across the
water and calm streaks along the wind. Octaves finer than a few
pixels are not drawn: their slope variance roughens the reflection, so
distant water blurs and glints rather than aliases, and their noise is
not computed.

Materials using any of it draw with a WATER variant of their program:
the pen twin (#define PEN) becomes ShaderProgram::variant(flags), so
other materials run their shaders unchanged. The water fields go in
with transmission, one update: each new material state takes an id,
and ids order the draws, so other materials keep theirs. With the
valley's water in the world the forest's pinned views render
bit-identical (TAA off; the drone view within its run-to-run noise).
A view without the scene copy sees clear water.

Measured (tools/render_forest.py views of the valley, frame log, GTX
1060, 1920x1080, against the same renderer's previous flat water):
GPU per frame, lake filling the view 15.37 -> 15.67 ms, lower over the
trees 35.76 -> 36.06 ms, high and sun-facing views within noise; frame
rates unchanged.
